r given too much of  either so part of survival is spending  these wisely while the onus is on you to  figure out the best use case scenario  for these items and upgrades many of  these elements aren't always abundantly  clear the more you experiment with  strafe the more Easter eggs and secrets  begin to reveal themselves jump to the  first level without choosing a gun and a  wrench will be your primary weapon play  the Wolfenstein 3d clone arcade machine  and upgrades have spit out one  particular highlight was finding the  superhot shotgun the game itself turns  into superhot where only time moves as  you move up until it runs out of ammo  Easter eggs like this instill the desire  to find more secrets and go beyond  simply finishing the final level  [Music]  though the start and tail end of each  level remains the same large portions  are procedurally generated drawing from  a handful of preset rooms rearranged in  sequence and orientation while this  keeps you guessing to an extent with  each run familiarity starts to creep in  if there's a fault here it's that stray  fails to introduce truly unexpected  challenges thankfully the game's  redeeming qualities are enough to keep  you hooked and one of the strongest  hooks is the soundtrack sometimes you  get the urge to hop into the game just  to listen to these songs as if you  ordered music with a side of gameplay  [Music]  level 3 - - is a dark and frightening  place with the music to match the  blaring SIF melody over a catchy rhythm  coalesce with up-tempo beat and  industrial percussions that make the  song grimy and horrifying and inspiring  all at the same time level 2 - one is  your first encounter with open air to  relieve the claustrophobia of the first  levels as you mow down new enemies a  sense of wonder excitement and  desperation is instilled by the  infectious electronic rock track that  you can't stop humming or get out of  your head  moments of chaos are bookended by the  tranquil and ethereal tracks in each  exit room and Shop the music never loses  its grip and it never disappoints and it  becomes part of straights personality  adding a significant layer of enjoyment  though the first levels of Icarus feel  pulled straight out of the original doom  with its tight corridors and dim  lighting you begin to see subsequent  levels open up and tie together the low  five retro aesthetic is colourful and  clean which makes for both silly and  terrifying enemies at splattered  excessive gore and literally paint the  town red any semblance of story is told  through the environment alone and it's  one of the aspects that make the game  alluring it makes for quirky and varied  set pieces for frantic shooting and it's  enough to lead you along to the  satisfying conclusion  however the game isn't without its  technical issues enemies occasionally  shoot at you through walls or glitch out  and zip across a room and either be gone  entirely or sneak up behind you to cause  unnecessary damage later levels had a  few inexplicable framedrops thankfully  these issues are rare enough as to not  entirely ruin an otherwise refined  experience as unforgiving repetitive and  frustrating as it can be the urge to  jump back into the game and take out  that frustration on the hordes of  enemies to the tune of the most proper  soundtrack with a toybox of guns is hard  to resist  straight where's its influence is on its  sleeve but stands out on its own as a  fun intense and fast-paced shooter with  distinguishable charisma wow we've never  seen results this impressive before  you're more than ready to travel to the  edge of the galaxy  [Music]  you
 

